  "query_preview": "..\nFriday: Elevated to critical fire weather conditions are \nexpected over the next 24 hours for portions of the Inland \nNorthwest. Another round of showers and thunderstorms are \ndeveloping on radar and satellite with a deepening cu field in \neastern Washington and north Idaho already. There is ample \ninstability leftover from today (1400 J/kg on the 0Z KOTX \nsounding) to tap into still with steep lapse rates aloft. \nSynoptic ascent is maximized towards the Cascades with the \nupper low just offshore the Washington border, but a weak vort \nmax will develop off the foothills of the Blue Mountains in the \nnext couple of hours supporting additional development further \neast in eastern Washington. Red flag warnings continue for much \nof the region outside of the Columbia Basin and Chelan county \nfor abundant lightning until 7-11 AM this morning. Cannot rule \nout small hail up to penny size and gusty outflow winds up to 40\nmph with these storms. Storm coverage will decrease in the late\nmorning as they mostly migrate into far southeastern BC. \nExpecting one more round of more isolated showers and \nthunderstorms Friday afternoon (10-25% chance for the central \nPanhandle mountains) but the coverage of storms is too low to \nwarrant another lightning driven red flag warning. Further west \nthis afternoon, a dry cold front passage will occur and breezy \nsoutherly/southeasterly winds will pick up for the Okanogan \nValley, Methow Valley, and Waterville Plateau, leading to \ncritical fire weather conditions. The Waterville Plateau Red \nFlag Warning will go a couple hours longer until 11 PM as the \nevening gap winds bring a wind shift to the west. \n\nSaturday: Additional elevated to locally critical fire weather \nconditions are expected Saturday as the Inland northwest sits \nsquarely in the dry slot of the upper level low with a \ntightening 500 mb height gradient. Winds will be west-southwest \n12-15 mph with gusts around 15-20 mph with relative humidity \naround 14-22 percent. Temperatures will be cooler but still \nabove average in the upper 80s to mid 90s with plentiful sun. \n\nSunday through Friday: Ensembles are in agreement of well above \nnormal temperatures continuing next week as the southwest regains \nstrength and retrogrades towards the 4 corners region by Monday. \nConditions will be fairly dry with highs in the 90s and 100s.\n/DB",
